D069 (founder request, 2026-11-13; F6; wiki/decisions/D069-openai-ads-measurement.md): the order remembers which ChatGPT
ad click brought the buyer. One import and the `fb_attrib:` line: `withOpenAiAttrib(readFbAttrib(request), request)` merges
`oai_oppref` and `oai_obref`, read from the request's cookies and validated, into the SAME `fb_attrib` JSONB Meta's
attribution already lives in. There is NO new column and NO migration (a migration is applied by hand, and code that
inserted a new column ahead of it would answer every pre-order with a 500); Meta's reader picks only its own four keys.
`fb_attrib` stays null only when both are empty. The amount, the tier, the validation, the order of operations, the rate
limit, the Razorpay call and every response are unchanged.

D075 (founder's choice, chat 26 Nov 2026; K-124 part b; wiki/decisions/D075-session-id-on-orders.md): the order remembers the
PostHog session id. One import (`readPhSessionId`, new file src/lib/tracking/ph-session-id.ts) and the `ph_session_id:` line
beside `ph_distinct_id:`: the id is read ON THE SERVER from the PostHog cookie the browser already sent (`$sesid`, validated
UUID-shaped and at most 64 characters, else null), so `purchase_confirmed` can carry it as `$session_id` and PostHog attributes
the sale to the visit's own entry referrer, landing path, click ids and UTMs. A missing, malformed or oversized cookie stores
null and never refuses an order. No client change, no new cookie, nothing new to Meta, GA4 or OpenAI. It needs the NEW column
from supabase/migrations/0005_ph_session_id.sql, applied by hand BEFORE this deploys (as 0004 was): code that inserted it
ahead of the migration would answer every pre-order with a 500. The amount, the tier, the validation, the order of operations,
the rate limit, the Razorpay call and every response are unchanged.

